a running call counts up while the daemon says nothing

R13. The operator, 2026-09-21: *"when a tool call takes time — say `cargo build` —
it is frozen at 0ms until it finishes. A live coarse timer would be nice, say
1/10th of a second."*

## First: this head's defect is NOT leticl's, and the difference is the whole case

leticl measured its own half and reported that the **number was never the problem,
the asking was**: its loop paints only when a frame or a key marks the head dirty,
so anything computed from the clock was computed and never drawn. If that had been
true here, this commit would be about the draw loop and my `0ms`, my spinner and
`notice_ttl` would have been one defect with three symptoms.

**It is not true here, and that is checkable rather than arguable.** `term.keys()`
returns after ~100 ms of quiet (`VMIN=0 VTIME=1`), and `Driver::tick` composes a
frame on every pass — `let screen = app.screen(size.0, size.1);` is called
unconditionally, after the drain and before the ack — so a quiet screen is still a
frame every 100 ms and every clock-derived value in it is recomputed. That is also
why `notice_ttl` is **not** one of the symptoms: it counts frames, and frames tick.
(The spinner already read `App::now_ms` for the same reason; that its comment
explains the switch off `t.last_ms` is the clue to the real defect.)

## The real defect: two clocks, two hundred lines apart, on one screen

    turn_status   →  self.now_ms          ← advanced every tick by the driver
    call_card     →  TurnPane::last_ms    ← the last EVENT's timestamp

`last_ms` **stops** whenever the daemon stops saying things — which is precisely
what a silent `cargo build` does. So a row carried a spinner that turned and a
duration frozen at `0ms`, because they were reading different clocks: the border
said `Responding · 30.0s` while the card directly above it said `0ms`. Both numbers
were "measured"; only one of them was measuring anything.

## The fix, and why it is an anchor rather than a substitution

Using `self.now_ms` for the running card is the obvious fix and it is wrong for one
case: a `--replay` applies every frame **before** it ever sets a clock (`bin`:
the envelopes are applied in a loop; `app.clock` is called only in the interactive
loop after them), so its frames carry the recording's timestamps while `now_ms` is
whatever the head was last told. Mixing the two would date a year-old call from
today — the same class of error as the `Responding · 496940h16m` a snapshot used to
render, which this file already has a guard for.

So `CallRow` gains `started_at`: **the head's own clock at the moment the call
started, or 0 when the head had no clock then.** The elapsed is measured from that
when it exists, and from the log's span when it does not, and the anchor is taken
*when the event is handled* — not at render — for exactly the reason above: in a
replay there is no clock to take yet, so the anchor records the absence honestly
instead of guessing at render time.

**And it moves wherever `started_ms` moves**, which is the half that is easy to get
wrong and was: `started_ms` restarts at `ToolStarted` because *"a call that waited
on a decision did not spend that time running"*, so `started_at` restarts there too.
My first cut anchored only at the proposal, which silently counted the guard's
decision wait as running time — the exact sentence that comment exists to prevent,
arrived at from the other side. Found by the test, whose first run read `200ms` where
it wanted `1.0s`.

## R13's two-clocks trap, checked in the second place it could live

leticl found a second instance while building §1.6: its secret card subtracted a
**Unix** deadline from a **monotonic** clock and had been drawing a number in the
hundreds of thousands of seconds since it was written. The operator asked me to
check the third possible one, and the answer is **this head does not have it** — the
deadline on the wire is `letibot_sessionlog::event::now_ms()` (epoch), and the clock
this head is handed (`bin::now_ms`, `driver::now_ms`) is the epoch too, so the
subtraction is in one frame.

That was a property nobody had asserted, which is the kind of thing that stops being
true quietly, so it is a test now rather than a sentence. It also turned up a small
fact worth keeping: the countdown is the **last** field on that line and the card
*trims to the width*, so on a 96-column terminal the number is cut off before it can
be read. Not fixed here — recorded, because a deadline a narrow screen cannot show is
the same family as everything else this queue is about.

## Tests

- `a_running_call_counts_up_while_the_daemon_says_nothing` — the whole requirement,
  and it asserts its own premise: `last_event_at` is captured and re-checked at both
  assertions, so the test cannot pass because an event happened to arrive. 200 ms in
  with nothing said the row reads `200ms`; thirty silent seconds later it reads
  `30.0s`. The second half is the case that must **not** be lost: a head that was
  never told the time keeps the log's own span (`assert_eq!(b.now_ms, 0)` first), so
  the replay is pinned rather than assumed.
- `the_password_cards_countdown_is_seconds_and_not_a_second_epoch` — the two-clocks
  check above, with the daemon's own shape (`now_ms + PATIENCE`) and a countdown
  asserted at two instants.
- Three existing fixtures updated for the new field (`edit_row`, the snapshot
  rebuild, and the secret card's own test, which asserts against a wide frame for the
  reason above).

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **301 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (299 + 2).
All targets: 3 + 3 passed.

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` on `crates/tui/src/
app.rs` did **not** run this time — the check needs to write a temp file, and `bash`
is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker (three earlier refusals, from the
corpus reads; only the operator lifts it). `cargo test` compiled and ran the crate,
so the code is valid; the formatting is written to rustfmt's shape by hand and has
not been diffed. `cargo check --workspace` also cannot run: `/home/dead/Projects/
rano` — a path dependency — has an unclosed delimiter in `src/syntax.rs`, somebody
else's edit in flight, unrelated to this tree.

## Not in this commit

**R16** is next and is the same root cause this file's `bound_prompts` started from:
an echo retires on `TranscriptContent` matched by text, and a compaction *replaces*
the transcript, so the event it waits for never arrives in the form it expects. Then
**R14**, **R12**, **§1.6** — for which leticl's `550bc94` is the reference, and
whose secret-card bug is the one checked above.

**leticl's half of R13** is its own tree's; its finding is recorded here because it
is the reason this commit opens with "not leticl's defect" rather than with the loop.
